		{"id":"2ab16ce696335cc1","x":-520,"y":-380,"width":1020,"height":720,"type":"text","text":"  \n\n##### You said:\n\nself-exciting Hawkes process what does this mean, in reference to system 2 for recommendation systems\n\n  \n\n###### ChatGPT said:\n\nA **self-exciting Hawkes process** is a type of mathematical model used to describe events that **increase the likelihood of more events happening soon after**.\n\nLet’s break this down and then connect it to **System 2 in recommendation systems**.\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ What is a Hawkes Process?\n\nA **Hawkes process** is a _self-exciting point process_.\n\nThat means:\n\n- Events happen over time (clicks, views, purchases, etc.)\n    \n- Each event temporarily **raises the probability** of more events happening soon\n    \n- The effect **decays over time**\n    \n\nThink of it like:\n\n> One click makes another click more likely.  \n> One retweet makes more retweets more likely.  \n> One purchase increases short-term interest in related items.\n\nMathematically, the event rate (intensity) increases after each event and then slowly falls back to baseline.\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Why “Self-Exciting”?\n\n“Self-exciting” means:\n\n> Events cause more events.\n\nExample outside recommendation systems:\n\n- Earthquakes → aftershocks\n    \n- Viral posts → rapid cascades of shares\n    \n- Financial trades → bursts of market activity\n    \n\nEach event temporarily boosts future intensity.\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ What This Means in Recommendation Systems\n\nIn recommendation systems, a Hawkes process models **user activity bursts and cascades**.\n\nFor example:\n\nIf a user:\n\n- Watches one video\n    \n- Then watches another\n    \n- Then clicks a related item\n    \n\nEach interaction increases the short-term probability of another interaction.\n\nSo instead of assuming:\n\n> “User interest is static”\n\nThe Hawkes model assumes:\n\n> “User interest dynamically spikes after interactions”\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ What Is “System 2” in Recommendation Systems?\n\nIn many recommender architectures, teams informally split systems into:\n\n- **System 1** → Fast, reactive, short-term signals\n    \n- **System 2** → Slower, strategic, longer-term reasoning\n    \n\nThis terminology is inspired by the dual-process theory popularized by Thinking, Fast and Slow.\n\nIn recommendation systems:\n\n- **System 1**: Immediate behavioral signals (recent clicks)\n    \n- **System 2**: Structured modeling of user intent, exploration, diversity, long-term value\n    \n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ Hawkes Process in “System 2” Context\n\nWhen someone refers to a:\n\n> “Self-exciting Hawkes process in System 2”\n\nThey usually mean:\n\n### Instead of just reacting to clicks…\n\nSystem 2 **explicitly models temporal dynamics of interest**.\n\nIt does things like:\n\n- Model **interest bursts**\n    \n- Separate baseline preference from short-term excitation\n    \n- Predict when engagement will decay\n    \n- Model cascading consumption behavior\n    \n\nFor example:\n\nUser watches:\n\n- 1 cooking video\n    \n- Then 3 more in 20 minutes\n    \n\nHawkes model says:\n\n- First view raises intensity\n    \n- Each new view increases it more\n    \n- The intensity decays over time\n    \n- System predicts when the session ends\n    \n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ Why This Is Useful\n\nWithout Hawkes modeling:\n\n- Systems may overfit to recent clicks\n    \n- Or fail to recognize bursty behavior\n    \n- Or miss session dynamics\n    \n\nWith Hawkes modeling:\n\n- Better session modeling\n    \n- More accurate next-item prediction\n    \n- Better notification timing\n    \n- Better re-engagement targeting\n    \n\n---\n\n## 7️⃣ Intuition Summary\n\nThink of it like this:\n\n- Baseline interest = user’s long-term taste\n    \n- Excitation = short-term momentum\n    \n- Decay = fading attention\n    \n\nThe Hawkes process mathematically combines these.\n\n---\n\n## 8️⃣ Simple Analogy\n\nImagine a campfire:\n\n- Baseline heat = long-term preference\n    \n- Each log added = interaction\n    \n- Fire flares up = increased probability of more interactions\n    \n- Fire slowly cools = decay\n    \n\nThat’s a self-exciting process.\n\n---\n\nIf you'd like, I can also explain:\n\n- The mathematical formula (intensity function λ(t))\n    \n- How it compares to RNN/Transformer session models\n    \n- How it’s implemented in production recommenders\n    \n- Or how it connects to reinforcement learning\n    \n\nJust tell me which direction you want to go.\n\n  \n\n##### You said:\n\nexplain all this in short and layman terms\n\n  \n\n###### ChatGPT said:\n\nIn very simple terms:\n\nA **self-exciting Hawkes process** is a way to model behavior where **doing something makes you more likely to do it again soon**.\n\n### Everyday example\n\nIf you watch one cooking video, you're more likely to watch another right away.  \nAfter a while, that excitement fades.\n\nThat “boost-then-fade” pattern is exactly what a Hawkes process models.\n\n---\n\n## In recommendation systems\n\nPlatforms often think in two layers (inspired by Thinking, Fast and Slow):\n\n- **System 1** → Quick reactions (recent clicks)\n    \n- **System 2** → Smarter thinking about patterns over time\n    \n\nWhen someone says:\n\n> “Self-exciting Hawkes process in System 2”\n\nThey mean:\n\nInstead of just reacting to your last click, the system:\n\n- Knows that one action increases short-term interest\n    \n- Knows that interest fades over time\n    \n- Uses that pattern to predict what you’ll do next\n    \n\n---\n\n### Super short version\n\nIt’s a math model that says:\n\n> “If you just interacted with something, you’re temporarily more likely to interact again — but that boost fades.”\n\nThat’s it.\n\n  \n\n##### You said:\n\nwe propose a generative model of user arrival rates based on a self-exciting Hawkes process where the probability to return to the platform depends on their experiences during past sessions. There is substantial empirical evidence which suggests that a user’s return to the platform can also be driven by impulsive behavior in addition to utility-driven behavior (Cho et al., 2021; Moser, 2020; Lyngs et al., 2019b). Hence, inspired by the “dual system” model of Kleinberg et al. (2022), we consider the influence of both System-1 and System-2 decision-processes in modeling user return probability. In particular, the triggering kernel of the Hawkes process has two components: 1) A trigger intensity based on a System-1 process driven by the instantaneous gratification or moreishness from past interactions with a rapidly vanishing effect of the user’s return probability; 2) A trigger intensity based on System-2 process driven by the utility of past interactions and with a more steady longer-term impact on the user’s return probability. Our model allows for the possibility that a user keeps returning to the platform even when the platform always recommends moreish content because maybe the System-1 trigger is high enough for the user to keep returning (even though it lasts for a short while). However, we do not model long-term addictive behavior where the presence/absence of the user might not correlated with their experience on the platform. explain this in simple terms\n\n  \n\n###### ChatGPT said:\n\nHere’s what that paragraph is saying in plain English:\n\n---\n\n## Big Picture\n\nThey’re building a model to predict:\n\n> **When will a user come back to the platform?**\n\nAnd they assume that coming back is influenced by **past experiences on the platform**.\n\nThey use a **self-exciting Hawkes process**, which just means:\n\n> Each time a user visits, that visit can temporarily increase the chance they’ll visit again.\n\n---\n\n## Two Reasons People Come Back\n\nThe key idea is that users return for **two different psychological reasons**.\n\nInspired by dual-system thinking (from Thinking, Fast and Slow and later work), they split behavior into:\n\n### 1️⃣ System 1 — Impulsive / Emotional\n\nThis is:\n\n- Instant gratification\n    \n- “That was fun, I want more!”\n    \n- Short-term excitement\n    \n\nExample:  \nYou watch one funny video → it feels good → you come back soon.\n\nBut:\n\n- That excitement fades quickly.\n    \n- It doesn’t last long.\n    \n\nIn the model:\n\n- This effect spikes strongly\n    \n- Then disappears fast\n    \n\n---\n\n### 2️⃣ System 2 — Thoughtful / Utility-based\n\nThis is:\n\n- “This platform is useful.”\n    \n- “I learn things here.”\n    \n- “It helps me.”\n    \n\nExample:  \nYou use LinkedIn because it helps your career.  \nYou use a learning app because it improves skills.\n\nThis effect:\n\n- Is weaker moment-to-moment\n    \n- But lasts much longer\n    \n\nIn the model:\n\n- It builds steady long-term return probability.\n    \n\n---\n\n## What the Model Does\n\nWhen predicting whether you’ll return, the model combines:\n\n- 🔥 A short, strong “impulse” boost (System 1)\n    \n- 🧠 A slower, longer-lasting “value” boost (System 2)\n    \n\nTogether, these determine your return probability.\n\n---\n\n## Important Subtle Point\n\nThey allow for this situation:\n\n- The platform keeps recommending highly addictive / “moreish” content.\n    \n- System 1 keeps triggering short bursts of return.\n    \n- So users keep coming back repeatedly.\n    \n\nBut:\n\nThey are **not modeling full long-term addiction**, where users return even if their experience doesn’t matter anymore.\n\nSo they assume:\n\n> Your return is still connected to your past experiences.\n\nNot pure dependency or habit disconnected from value.\n\n---\n\n## Ultra Simple Summary\n\nThe model says:\n\n> People come back either because\n> \n> 1. it felt good (short-term impulse), or\n>     \n> 2. it was useful (long-term value).\n>     \n\nBoth effects influence return probability — but they fade at different speeds.\n\nThat’s it.\n\n  \n\n##### You said:\n\nThey are not modeling full long-term addiction, where users return even if their experience doesn’t matter anymore explain this in detail\n\n  \n\n###### ChatGPT said:\n\nGreat question — this is actually a very important modeling distinction.\n\nLet’s unpack it carefully.\n\n---\n\n# What They _Are_ Modeling\n\nTheir model assumes:\n\n> A user’s decision to return is driven by their past experiences on the platform.\n\nThat means:\n\n- If content feels good (System 1) → short-term boost in return probability\n    \n- If content is useful (System 2) → longer-term boost in return probability\n    \n- If experience is bad → return probability decreases\n    \n\nSo in their world:\n\n> Return behavior is tied to experience quality.\n\n---\n\n# What They Are _NOT_ Modeling: Full Long-Term Addiction\n\nWhen they say they are **not modeling full long-term addictive behavior**, they mean:\n\nThey are not modeling a situation where:\n\n> Users keep coming back even if the experience no longer matters.\n\nIn other words:\n\nReturn probability is still **experience-dependent**, not automatic.\n\n---\n\n# What Would “Full Addiction” Look Like in a Model?\n\nIn a true addiction model:\n\n1. Return becomes habit-based.\n    \n2. The act of returning reinforces itself.\n    \n3. The user may return regardless of whether content is good or bad.\n    \n\nMathematically, that would mean:\n\n- Return probability becomes self-sustaining.\n    \n- It no longer depends strongly on utility or gratification.\n    \n- There may be persistent state changes (e.g., habit formation).\n    \n\nIn such a case:\n\n> Even if content quality drops, the user still comes back.\n\nTheir model does _not_ allow that.\n\n---\n\n# Concrete Example\n\n### In Their Model\n\nIf:\n\n- Platform starts recommending low-quality content\n    \n- Utility drops\n    \n- Gratification drops\n    \n\nThen:\n\n- Both System 1 and System 2 triggers weaken\n    \n- Return probability decreases\n    \n\nSo behavior adjusts based on experience.\n\n---\n\n### In a True Addiction Model\n\nEven if:\n\n- Content becomes worse\n    \n- Utility decreases\n    \n\nThe user might still:\n\n- Open the app automatically\n    \n- Return due to habit\n    \n- Feel compelled to check it\n    \n\nReturn is no longer tightly linked to experience.\n\n---\n\n# Why This Distinction Matters\n\nTheir model assumes:\n\n> The platform influences return probability through experience.\n\nBut it does _not_ assume:\n\n- Habit formation independent of experience\n    \n- Compulsion dynamics\n    \n- Withdrawal effects\n    \n- Long-term psychological dependency\n    \n\nThose would require:\n\n- Additional state variables\n    \n- Persistent behavioral shifts\n    \n- Possibly nonlinear reinforcement loops\n    \n\n---\n\n# Technical Difference in Modeling Terms\n\nTheir Hawkes process:\n\n- Has excitation triggered by past sessions\n    \n- That excitation decays over time\n    \n- It is tied to past experience quality\n    \n\nA full addiction model might include:\n\n- Non-decaying reinforcement\n    \n- State transitions (casual → habitual → addicted)\n    \n- Structural change in baseline return rate\n    \n- Self-reinforcing loops independent of utility\n    \n\nThey are explicitly _not_ modeling those.\n\n---\n\n# Ultra Clear Summary\n\nThey assume:\n\n> “People return because past experiences influenced them.”\n\nThey do NOT assume:\n\n> “People return automatically, even if experiences stop mattering.”\n\nSo their model captures:\n\n- Impulse\n    \n- Value\n    \n- Short- and medium-term reinforcement\n    \n\nBut not:\n\n- Deep habit formation\n    \n- Psychological dependency\n    \n- Autonomous compulsion\n    \n\n---\n\nIf you'd like, I can also explain how a real “addiction-style” mathematical model would look different from this Hawkes-based setup.\n\n  \n\n##### You said:\n\nexplain with an example\n\n  \n\n###### ChatGPT said:\n\nLet’s make this very concrete with a simple story.\n\n---\n\n# Example: Sarah and a Short-Video App\n\n## Scenario 1 — What **their model** assumes (experience-driven return)\n\nSarah downloads a short-video app.\n\n### Week 1:\n\n- She watches funny, highly engaging videos.\n    \n- She enjoys them (System 1: instant gratification).\n    \n- She feels entertained and relaxed (System 2: perceived value).\n    \n\nBecause of that:\n\n> She comes back the next day.\n\nWhy?  \nBecause her **past experience was good**, which increased her probability of returning.\n\n---\n\n### Week 3:\n\nThe algorithm starts showing lower-quality videos.\n\nNow:\n\n- She’s less entertained.\n    \n- She feels it’s a waste of time.\n    \n\nIn their model:\n\n- The “impulse boost” (System 1) weakens.\n    \n- The “utility boost” (System 2) weakens.\n    \n- Her probability of returning decreases.\n    \n\nEventually:\n\n> She stops coming back.\n\nBecause return depends on experience quality.\n\n---\n\n# Scenario 2 — What “Full Addiction” Would Look Like (NOT modeled here)\n\nNow imagine a different version.\n\nEven if:\n\n- Videos become boring\n    \n- She complains about the app\n    \n- She says she doesn’t enjoy it anymore\n    \n\nShe still:\n\n- Automatically opens the app every morning\n    \n- Scrolls without thinking\n    \n- Feels uneasy if she doesn’t check it\n    \n\nIn this case:\n\n> Her return behavior is no longer strongly tied to experience.\n\nShe returns out of habit or compulsion — not because content is good.\n\nThat would require modeling:\n\n- Habit formation\n    \n- Behavioral dependency\n    \n- Possibly psychological reinforcement loops\n    \n\nTheir model does NOT include this.\n\n---\n\n# Key Difference in One Sentence\n\n**Their model:**  \n“Good experiences make you more likely to return.”\n\n**Addiction model:**  \n“You return even if the experience isn’t good anymore.”\n\n---\n\n# Even Simpler Example\n\nThink of coffee.\n\n- If it tastes good → you buy it again (experience-driven).\n    \n- If it tastes bad → you stop buying it.\n    \n\nThat’s their model.\n\nAddiction would be:\n\n- Even if it tastes bad, you still feel compelled to buy it.\n    \n\nThey are modeling the first case — not the second.\n\n---\n\nIf you want, I can also explain how this difference would appear mathematically in the Hawkes process.\n\n  \n\n##### You said:\n\nis there anything that can be improved in this, as another research work\n\n  \n\n###### ChatGPT said:\n\nYes — quite a lot can be extended or improved. Their model is clean and elegant, but it makes strong assumptions. That’s exactly where research opportunities lie.\n\nHere are meaningful directions for another research paper:\n\n---\n\n# 1️⃣ Model True Habit Formation (Long-Term State Change)\n\n**Limitation in their work:**  \nReturn probability always depends on recent experiences, and effects decay.\n\n**Extension idea:**  \nAdd a _persistent habit state_.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Users transition from:\n    \n    - Casual → Engaged → Habitual\n        \n- Baseline return rate increases permanently after repeated exposure.\n    \n- Return becomes partly independent of content quality.\n    \n\nThis would move from:\n\n> “Experience-driven return”\n\nTo:\n\n> “Experience + accumulated habit capital”\n\nThat’s publishable if formalized well.\n\n---\n\n# 2️⃣ Add Negative Feedback / Burnout Effects\n\nTheir model mainly focuses on positive excitation.\n\nBut in reality:\n\n- Overexposure can reduce return.\n    \n- Binge sessions can cause fatigue.\n    \n- Too much “moreish” content may reduce long-term utility.\n    \n\nYou could add:\n\n- **Inhibitory kernel components**\n    \n- A “satiation” effect\n    \n- Utility decay from excessive short-term stimulation\n    \n\nThat makes the model more realistic.\n\n---\n\n# 3️⃣ Model Heterogeneous Users\n\nRight now, System 1 and System 2 are probably fixed across users.\n\nYou could:\n\n- Cluster users into impulsive vs utility-driven types\n    \n- Learn user-specific decay rates\n    \n- Let the System-1 vs System-2 balance vary dynamically\n    \n\nExample:\n\n- Younger users → stronger System 1\n    \n- Professional users → stronger System 2\n    \n\nThis adds behavioral depth.\n\n---\n\n# 4️⃣ Make System 1 and System 2 Interact\n\nCurrently they are additive components.\n\nBut cognitively:\n\n- Too much System 1 may crowd out System 2.\n    \n- High utility may reduce impulsive use.\n    \n\nYou could model:\n\n- Nonlinear interaction terms\n    \n- Competition between triggers\n    \n- Threshold effects\n    \n\nThis makes the psychology more realistic.\n\n---\n\n# 5️⃣ Add Platform Policy Feedback Loop\n\nRight now:\n\nUser → Experience → Return\n\nBut platforms adapt recommendations based on behavior.\n\nYou could model:\n\nPlatform policy → Content type → System 1 / System 2 triggers → Return → Platform update\n\nThis becomes a dynamic game between:\n\n- User\n    \n- Algorithm\n    \n\nThat’s very powerful research territory.\n\n---\n\n# 6️⃣ Incorporate Reinforcement Learning\n\nInstead of passive modeling:\n\n- Use the Hawkes-based return model as part of an RL objective.\n    \n- Optimize recommendations to balance:\n    \n    - Short-term engagement\n        \n    - Long-term retention\n        \n    - Avoid addictive dynamics\n        \n\nThis ties modeling to optimization.\n\n---\n\n# 7️⃣ Ethical / Welfare Extensions\n\nYou could measure:\n\n- User welfare vs engagement\n    \n- When System-1 optimization harms long-term utility\n    \n- Tradeoff between profit and well-being\n    \n\nThat’s high-impact research right now.\n\n---\n\n# 8️⃣ Move Beyond Exponential Decay\n\nStandard Hawkes uses exponential decay.\n\nBut real behavior may show:\n\n- Power-law decay\n    \n- Memory effects\n    \n- Circadian cycles\n    \n\nImproving the kernel structure is a strong technical extension.\n\n---\n\n# 9️⃣ Add External Shocks\n\nReturn isn’t only driven by internal triggers.\n\nAdd:\n\n- Notifications\n    \n- Social signals\n    \n- Time-of-day effects\n    \n- Competing apps\n    \n\nThat makes the model more realistic.\n\n---\n\n# 10️⃣ Structural Causal Version\n\nRight now it’s predictive.\n\nYou could:\n\n- Identify causal effect of “moreish” content\n    \n- Estimate counterfactual return probability\n    \n- Model intervention policies\n    \n\nThat would be a major methodological contribution.\n\n---\n\n# Big-Picture Research Directions\n\nYou can push this work toward:\n\n- Behavioral economics\n    \n- Computational psychiatry (addiction modeling)\n    \n- Platform design ethics\n    \n- RL-based recommendation optimization\n    \n- Causal modeling\n    \n- Longitudinal habit formation\n    \n\n---\n\n# If You Want a Strong Research Angle\n\nThe most interesting and impactful extensions right now are:\n\n1. Habit formation (state-dependent baseline)\n    \n2. Welfare-aware optimization\n    \n3. Interaction between System 1 & System 2\n    \n4. Platform-user dynamic game modeling\n    \n\n---\n\nIf you'd like, tell me:\n\n- Are you approaching this from ML, economics, behavioral science, or CS?\n    \n- Is this for a thesis, paper, or critique?\n    \n\nI can suggest a more precise research proposal outline.\n\n  \n\n##### You said:\n\nso basically they have shown the existence of long term vs short term user engagements and how one can leverage them for better recommendations, while not considering the grey or noisy area in betwee which represents short term addiction which can lead to long term results\n\n  \n\n###### ChatGPT said:\n\nYes — that’s a very good intuition. Let’s refine it slightly so it’s precise.\n\n### What they _did_ show\n\nThey model two clean components of engagement:\n\n- **Short-term (System 1)**  \n    Fast, impulsive, “this feels good right now.”  \n    Strong effect, but fades quickly.\n    \n- **Long-term (System 2)**  \n    Slower, value-driven, “this is useful.”  \n    Weaker spike, but lasts longer.\n    \n\nSo their model separates:\n\n> Temporary excitement vs sustained value.\n\nAnd they show how both influence return probability.\n\n---\n\n### What they _do not_ model (your “grey zone”)\n\nYou’re pointing to something very important:\n\nThere may be a **middle region** where:\n\n- Repeated short-term gratification\n    \n- Accumulates over time\n    \n- Gradually changes behavior\n    \n- Eventually affects long-term return\n    \n\nThat is:\n\n> Short-term impulses might compound into long-term behavioral shifts.\n\nTheir model does **not** allow this transformation.\n\nIn their setup:\n\n- System 1 fades quickly.\n    \n- It does not permanently alter the baseline.\n    \n- It does not convert into habit.\n    \n\n---\n\n### What the “grey/noisy area” would represent\n\nThat grey area could represent:\n\n1. Repeated short bursts creating habit formation\n    \n2. Short-term “moreish” content slowly increasing baseline return rate\n    \n3. A dynamic where impulsive use gradually reshapes long-term preferences\n    \n\nIn other words:\n\n> System 1 may influence System 2 over time.\n\nTheir model keeps them separate.\n\n---\n\n### Why this matters\n\nIn reality:\n\n- Many platforms grow engagement through repeated short-term rewards.\n    \n- Over time, users may internalize routines.\n    \n- Short-term loops can generate long-term retention.\n    \n\nThat feedback loop is not captured in their framework.\n\n---\n\n### More accurate restatement of your insight\n\nInstead of saying they ignored addiction entirely, it’s more precise to say:\n\n> They model short-term and long-term engagement as separate effects, but do not model the dynamic pathway through which repeated short-term gratification may reshape long-term engagement or baseline return behavior.\n\nThat “conversion pathway” is exactly where strong new research could emerge.\n\n---\n\nIf you'd like, I can help you phrase this as a sharp academic critique suitable for a paper or literature review.\n\n  \n\n##### You said:\n\nTraditional recommender systems based on revealed preferences often fail to capture the fundamental duality in user behavior, where consumption choices are driven by both inherent value (enrichment) and instant appeal (temptation). Consequently, these systems may generate recommendations that prioritize short-term engagement over long-lasting user satisfaction. We propose a novel recommender design that explicitly models the tension between enrichment and temptation. We introduce a behavioral model that accounts for how both enrichment and temptation influence user choices, while incorporating the reality of off-platform alternatives. Building on this model, we formulate a novel recommendation objective aligned with maximizing consumed enrichment and prove the optimality of a locally greedy recommendation strategy. Finally, we present an estimation framework that leverages the distinction between explicit user feedback and implicit choice data while making minimal assumptions about off-platform options. Through comprehensive evaluation using both synthetic simulations and real-world data from the MovieLens dataset, we demonstrate that our approach consistently outperforms competitive baselines that ignore temptation dynamics either by assuming revealed preferences or recommending solely based on enrichment. Our work represents a paradigm shift toward more nuanced and user-centric recommender design, with significant implications for developing responsible AI systems that genuinely serve users’ long-term interests rather than merely maximizing engagement. explain this now, theis is the paper which used the prev one as one the related works and have built over them\n\n  \n\n###### ChatGPT said:\n\nGreat — this paper is basically saying:\n\n> “It’s not enough to model short-term vs long-term engagement. We should _design the recommender itself_ to favor long-term enrichment over temptation.”\n\nLet’s unpack it simply.\n\n---\n\n# 1️⃣ The Problem They See\n\nTraditional recommender systems assume:\n\n> “If a user clicks or watches something, that means they like it.”\n\nThis is called **revealed preference**.\n\nBut the authors argue:\n\nThat’s too simplistic.\n\nBecause user choices are driven by two forces:\n\n- 🎓 **Enrichment** → Things that are good for you long-term  \n    (learning, meaningful content, growth)\n    \n- 🍭 **Temptation** → Things that feel good immediately  \n    (clickbait, addictive short videos, junk content)\n    \n\nA user may _choose_ temptation even if enrichment is better for them.\n\nSo clicks ≠ true long-term preference.\n\n---\n\n# 2️⃣ What They Propose\n\nThey build a recommender that explicitly models:\n\n> The tension between enrichment and temptation.\n\nInstead of assuming all engagement is equal, they say:\n\nEach item has two properties:\n\n- How enriching it is\n    \n- How tempting it is\n    \n\nUser choices result from a mix of both.\n\n---\n\n# 3️⃣ Important Addition: Off-Platform Alternatives\n\nThey also recognize something realistic:\n\nUsers don’t just choose between items on your platform.\n\nThey can:\n\n- Close the app\n    \n- Watch Netflix\n    \n- Go outside\n    \n- Use another app\n    \n\nSo they include:\n\n> The possibility that users leave instead of consuming low-quality content.\n\nThis makes the model more realistic.\n\n---\n\n# 4️⃣ What Is the Goal Now?\n\nMost recommenders optimize:\n\n> Maximize engagement (clicks, watch time).\n\nThis paper changes the objective:\n\n> Maximize consumed enrichment.\n\nThat is a big philosophical shift.\n\nThey’re saying:\n\nThe algorithm should prioritize what benefits the user long-term — not what maximizes short-term attention.\n\n---\n\n# 5️⃣ Their Key Result\n\nThey prove something interesting:\n\nA **locally greedy strategy** (choosing the best enriching item at each step) is optimal under their model.\n\nThat’s powerful because:\n\n- It’s simple\n    \n- It’s implementable\n    \n- It aligns with long-term welfare\n    \n\n---\n\n# 6️⃣ How They Estimate It\n\nThey separate:\n\n- Explicit feedback → ratings (what users say they like)\n    \n- Implicit behavior → clicks / viewing\n